\begin_layout Section
Foundations
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Sets
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Basic notions
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
In mathematics,
 a set is a collection of different things,
 called elements or members of the set.
 A set may also be called a 
\emph on
collection
\emph default
 or 
\emph on
family
\emph default
,
 especially when its elements are themselves sets;
 this may avoid confusion between the set and its members.
 A set may be specified either by listing its elements or by giving a property that characterizes its elements,
 such as for the set of the prime numbers or the set of all students in a given class.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
If 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 is an element of a set 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

,
 one says that 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 belongs to 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 or 
\emph on
is in
\emph default
 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

,
 and one writes 
\begin_inset Formula $x\in A$
\end_inset

.
 The statement 
\emph on

\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 is not in 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset


\emph default
 is written a 
\begin_inset Formula $x\notin A$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The 
\emph on
axiom of extensionality
\emph default
 states that two sets are equal if and only if they have the same elements,
 or
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
A=B\Longleftrightarrow\forall x(x\in A\Longleftrightarrow x\in B).
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
There exists a set with no elements,
 and extensionality implies that there is only one such set.
 It is called the 
\emph on
empty set
\emph default
 (or null set) and is denoted 
\begin_inset Formula $\varnothing$
\end_inset

 or {}.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
A singleton is a set with exactly one element.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 is this element,
 the singleton is denoted 
\begin_inset Formula $\{x\}$
\end_inset

.
 The sets 
\begin_inset Formula $\{\varnothing\}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\varnothing$
\end_inset

 are different,
 because the former has one element (namely 
\begin_inset Formula $\varnothing$
\end_inset

) and the latter has no elements at all.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Specifying a set
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Extensionality implies that to specify a set,
 it suffices either to list its elements or to provide a property that characterize the set's elements among the elements of a possibly larger set.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Minisec
Roster notation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition

\emph on
Roster
\emph default
 or 
\emph on
enumeration notation
\emph default
 is a notation introduced by Ernst Zermelo in 1908 that specifies a set by listing its elements between braces,
 separated by commas.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Example
The notations 
\begin_inset Formula $\{\}$
\end_inset

 for the empty set and 
\begin_inset Formula $\{x\}$
\end_inset

 for a singleton are examples of roster notation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
When specifying a set,
 all that matters is whether each potential element is in the set or not,
 so a set does not change if elements are repeated or arranged in a different order.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
When there is a clear pattern for generating all set elements,
 one can use an ellipsis to abbreviate the notation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Minisec
Set–builder notation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
Set–builder notation specifies a set as being the set of all elements that satisfy some logical formula.
 More precisely,
 if 
\begin_inset Formula $\varpi(x)$
\end_inset

 is a logical formula depending on a variable 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

,
 which evalutates to 
\begin_inset Formula $\top$
\end_inset

 or 
\begin_inset Formula $\bot$
\end_inset

 depending on the value of 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

,
 then
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\{x:\varpi(x)\}
\]

\end_inset

denotes the set of all 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 for which 
\begin_inset Formula $\varpi(x)$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $\top$
\end_inset

.
 In this notation,
 
\begin_inset Formula $:$
\end_inset

 is read as 
\emph on
such that
\emph default
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Set relations and operations
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
There are two types of things one can do with sets:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
One can compare sets and see their relationship.
 These are called 
\emph on
set relations
\emph default
.
 Equality is the first such relation encountered this far.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
One can produce new sets from other sets.
 These are called 
\emph on
set operations
\emph default
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Subsets
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
As seen before,
 sets 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 are equal iff they have the same elements,
 denoted as 
\begin_inset Formula $A=B$
\end_inset

.
 Equality is reflexive;
 for every set 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $B=B$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
A 
\emph on
subset
\emph default
 of a set 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 is a set 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 such that every element of 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 is also an element of 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

,
 or
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
A\subseteq B\Longleftrightarrow\forall x(x\in A\Longrightarrow x\in B).
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The relationship between sets established by 
\begin_inset Formula $\subseteq$
\end_inset

 is called inclusion or containment.
 Containment is reflexive too;
 every set 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 is a subset of itself,
 namely 
\begin_inset Formula $B\subseteq B$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
A set 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 is a 
\emph on
proper subset
\emph default
 of a set 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 if 
\begin_inset Formula $A\subseteq B$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $A\neq B$
\end_inset

;
 to denote this one writes 
\begin_inset Formula $A\subset B$
\end_inset

,
 therefore
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
A\subset B\Longleftrightarrow A\subseteq B\wedge A\neq B.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Minisec
Properties of containment
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Containment is antisymmetric;
 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall A,B$
\end_inset

 set,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
A=B\Longleftrightarrow A\subseteq B\wedge A\supseteq B.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The empty set is a subset of every set:
 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall A$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\varnothing\subseteq A$
\end_inset

.
 In particular,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall A\neq\varnothing$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\varnothing\subset A$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "subsec:Basic-operations"

\end_inset

Internal operations
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
There are several standard operations that produce new sets from given sets,
 analogously to how addition and multiplication produce new numbers from given numbers.
 The operations that are considered in this section are those such that all elements of the produced sets belong to a previously defined set.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Minisec
Intersection
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
The 
\emph on
intersection
\emph default
 of sets 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 is set denoted 
\begin_inset Formula $A\cap B$
\end_inset

 whose elements are those elements that belong to both 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

.
 That is,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
A\cap B=\{x:x\in A\wedge x\in B\}.
\]

\end_inset

Therefore 
\begin_inset Formula $A\cap B\subseteq A$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $A\cap B\subseteq B$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Intersection is associative and commutative;
 this means that for proceeding a sequence of intersections,
 one may proceed in any order,
 without the need of parentheses for specifying the order of operations.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
If 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{S}$
\end_inset

 is a nonempty set of sets,
 its intersection,
 denoted
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\bigcap\nolimits\mathcal{S}=\bigcap\nolimits_{A\in\mathcal{S}}A=\{x:(\forall A\in\mathcal{S})x\in A\},
\]

\end_inset

is the set whose elements are those elements that belong to all sets in 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{S}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Minisec
Union
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
The 
\emph on
union
\emph default
 of sets 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 is a set denoted 
\begin_inset Formula $A\cup B$
\end_inset

 whose elements are those elements that belong to 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 or 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 (or both).
 That is,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
A\cup B=\{x|x\in A\vee x\in B\}.
\]

\end_inset

Therefore 
\begin_inset Formula $A\subseteq A\cup B$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $B\subseteq A\cup B$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Union is associative and commutative.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
If 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{S}$
\end_inset

 is a set of sets,
 its union,
 denoted
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\bigcup\mathcal{S}=\bigcup\nolimits_{A\in\mathcal{S}}A=\{x|(\exists A\in\mathcal{S})x\in A\},
\]

\end_inset

is the set whose elements are those elementes that belong to at least one set in 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{S}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Minisec
Set difference
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
The 
\emph on
set difference
\emph default
 of set 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 from 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

,
 is a set,
 denoted 
\begin_inset Formula $A\setminus B$
\end_inset

,
 whose elements are those elements that belong to 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

,
 but not to 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

.
 That is,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
A\setminus B=\{x|x\in A\wedge x\notin B\}.
\]

\end_inset

Note that 
\begin_inset Formula $A\setminus B=A\setminus(A\cap B)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
When 
\begin_inset Formula $B\subseteq A$
\end_inset

 the difference 
\begin_inset Formula $A\setminus B=\neg_{A}B$
\end_inset

 is also called the 
\emph on
complement
\emph default
 of 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 in 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

.
 Naively speaking,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\neg B=\{x|x\notin B\},
\]

\end_inset

but this does not lead to a set as is.
 When all sets that are considered are subsets of a fixed 
\emph on
universal set
\emph default
 
\begin_inset Formula $U$
\end_inset

,
 the complement 
\begin_inset Formula $\neg_{U}A$
\end_inset

 is often called the absolute complement of 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

,
 and is denoted simply as 
\begin_inset Formula $\neg A$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Informally however,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
A\setminus B=A\cap\neg B.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
The 
\emph on
symmetric difference
\emph default
 of sets 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

,
 denoted 
\begin_inset Formula $A\bigtriangleup B$
\end_inset

,
 is the set of those elements that belong to 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 or 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 but not on both:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
A\bigtriangleup B=(A\setminus B)\cup(B\setminus A)=(A\cup B)\setminus(A\cap B).
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Functions
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
A 
\emph on
function
\emph default
 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 from a set 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 to a set 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 is a rule that assigns to each element of 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 a unique element of 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The notation 
\begin_inset Formula $f:A\to B$
\end_inset

 denotes a function 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 from 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 to 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

.The result of applying 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 to an element 
\begin_inset Formula $x\in A$
\end_inset

 is denoted 
\begin_inset Formula $f(x)$
\end_inset

;
 it is called the 
\emph on
value
\emph default
 of 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 at 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

,
 or the 
\emph on
image
\emph default
 of 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 under 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

.
 The set 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 is called the 
\emph on
domain
\emph default
 of 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

,
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 is called the 
\emph on
codomain
\emph default
 of 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

.
 The set 
\begin_inset Formula $f(A)\subseteq B$
\end_inset

 called the image set of 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

,
 is the subset of the codomain every element of which is a mapped element from 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
f(A)=\{f(x):x\in A\}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
Special types of a function 
\begin_inset Formula $f:A\to B$
\end_inset

:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Description

\emph on
injective
\emph default
 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall x,y\in A$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $f(x)=f(y)\Rightarrow x=y$
\end_inset

,
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description

\emph on
surjective
\emph default
 
\begin_inset Formula $f(A)=B$
\end_inset

,
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description

\emph on
bijective
\emph default
 is both injective and surjective.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Minisec
Indexed family
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Intuitively,
 an indexed family is a collection whose elements are labelled with the elements of an arbitrary (index) set 
\begin_inset Formula $I$
\end_inset

.
 These labels allow the same element to occur several times in the family.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
An indexed family is a function that has an index set 
\begin_inset Formula $I$
\end_inset

 as its domain.
 Generally,
 the usual functional notation is not used for indexed families.
 Instead,
 the element 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 of the index set 
\begin_inset Formula $I$
\end_inset

 is written as a subscript of the same name of the family.
 Special cases include:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $I=\mathbb{B}=\{\bot,\top\}$
\end_inset

 defines an ordered pair,
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $I=\mathbb{N}_{n-1}=\{0,...,n-1\}$
\end_inset

 (
\begin_inset Formula $\forall n\in\mathbb{N}$
\end_inset

) defines an 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

-tuple,
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $I=\mathbb{N}$
\end_inset

 defines a sequence.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Standard
Family of sets operations become indexed operations:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather*}
\bigcup\nolimits_{i\in I}A_{i}=\{x:\exists i\in I,x\in A_{i}\},\\
\bigcap\nolimits_{i\in I}A_{i}=\{x:\forall i\in I,x\in A_{i}\}.
\end{gather*}

\end_inset

For intersection,
 
\begin_inset Formula $I\neq\varnothing$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Minisec
Indicator function
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
Given a set 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall B\subseteq A$
\end_inset

,
 its 
\emph on
indicator function
\emph default
 is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mathds{1}_{B}:A\to\mathbb{B}:x\mapsto\mathds{1}_{B}(x)=\begin{cases}
\top & x\in A,\\
\bot & x\notin A.
\end{cases}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
External operations
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand formatted
reference "subsec:Basic-operations"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

,
 all elements of sets produced by set operations belong to previously defined sets.
 In this section,
 other set operations are considered,
 which produce sets whose elements can be outside all previously considered sets.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Minisec
Cartesian product
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
Given sets 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

,
 their 
\emph on
Cartesian product
\emph default
 (or simply 
\emph on
product
\emph default
),
 denoted 
\begin_inset Formula $A\times B$
\end_inset

,
 is the set of all ordered pairs 
\begin_inset Formula $(a,b)$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $a\in A$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $b\in B$
\end_inset

;
 that is,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
A\times B=\{(a,b)|a\in A\wedge b\in B\}.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The definition makes sense even if 
\begin_inset Formula $A=B$
\end_inset

,
 in which case we write 
\begin_inset Formula $A\times A=A^{2}$
\end_inset

.
 The graph of a function 
\begin_inset Formula $f:A\to B$
\end_inset

 is the set of all ordered pairs 
\begin_inset Formula $(a,f(a))$
\end_inset

 as 
\begin_inset Formula $a$
\end_inset

 ranged over all elements of 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

.
 It is a subset of the Cartesian product 
\begin_inset Formula $A\times B$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
Given an indexed family of sets 
\begin_inset Formula $(A_{i})_{i\in I}$
\end_inset

,
 the product
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\prod\nolimits_{i\in I}A_{i}
\]

\end_inset

is the set of all indexed families of elements 
\begin_inset Formula $(a_{i})_{i\in I}$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall i\in I$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{i}\in A_{i}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Minisec
Set exponentiation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
Given two sets 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

,
 the 
\emph on
set exponentiation
\emph default
,
 denoted 
\begin_inset Formula $B^{A}$
\end_inset

,
 is the set that has as elements all functions 
\begin_inset Formula $A\rightarrow B$
\end_inset

,
 i.e.:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
B^{A}=\{f:f:A\rightarrow B\}.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Equivalently,
 
\begin_inset Formula $B^{A}$
\end_inset

 can be viewed as the Cartesian product of a family,
 indexed by 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

,
 of sets that are all equal to 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

.
 This explains the terminology and the notation,
 since exponentiation with integer exponents is a product where all factors are equal to the base:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
B^{A}=\prod\nolimits_{a\in A}B=\{(b_{a})_{a\in A}|\forall a\in A,b_{a}\in B\}.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Cardinality
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
Informally,
 the cardinality of a set 
\begin_inset Formula $S$
\end_inset

,
 often denoted 
\begin_inset Formula $|S|$
\end_inset

,
 is the number of its members.
 This number is the natural number 
\begin_inset Formula $n\in\mathbb{N}$
\end_inset

 when there is a one-to-one correspondence between the set 
\begin_inset Formula $S$
\end_inset

 and the set 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{N}_{n}=\{1,2,...,n\}$
\end_inset

 of the first 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

 natural numbers.
 Also,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\vert\varnothing\vert=0.
\]

\end_inset

A set with the cardinality of a natural number is called a finite set,
 which applies in both cases.
 Otherwise,
 one has an infinite set.
 Having the same cardinality of a proper subset is a characteristic property of infinite sets:
 a set is infinite if and only if it has the same cardinality as one of its proper subsets.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
Infinite sets may have the same cardinalities still;
 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall A,B$
\end_inset

 sets,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\vert A\vert=\vert B|$
\end_inset

 iff 
\begin_inset Formula $\exists f:A\to B$
\end_inset

 bijective.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Relations
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In mathematics,
 a binary relation denotes some kind of relationship between two objects in a set,
 which may or may not hold.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
Given a set 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

,
 a binary relation 
\begin_inset Formula $\bigcirc$
\end_inset

 over 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

 is a set of ordered pairs of elements from 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

,
 formally 
\begin_inset Formula $\bigcirc\subseteq X\times X$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The statement 
\begin_inset Formula $(x,y)\in\bigcirc$
\end_inset

 reads in infix notation as 
\begin_inset Formula $x\bigcirc y$
\end_inset

.
 The order of the elements is important;
 if 
\begin_inset Formula $x\neq y$
\end_inset

 the 
\begin_inset Formula $y\bigcirc x$
\end_inset

 can be 
\begin_inset Formula $\top$
\end_inset

 or 
\begin_inset Formula $\bot$
\end_inset

 independently of 
\begin_inset Formula $x\bigcirc y$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

